To detect when a user scrolls to an element with a specific class and then add another class to it using **vanilla JavaScript**, we can utilize the **Intersection Observer API**. This API allows us to efficiently observe when an element enters or exits the viewport, without relying on scroll or resize events, which can be performance-intensive.

---

### ✅ Goal

- Observe elements with a class `target-class`.
- When such an element becomes visible in the viewport (i.e., intersects with it), add a class `active-class`.
- Ensure the class is added **only once**, even if the element scrolls in and out of view.

---

### 🧩 Key Concepts

- **`IntersectionObserver`**: A browser API that watches for changes in the intersection of a target element with an ancestor element or the viewport.
- **`threshold`**: A ratio from 0 to 1 at which crossing notifications are queued; an initial notification is also delivered.
- **`entry.isIntersecting`**: A boolean indicating whether the element is currently intersecting the viewport.

---

### 🧑‍💻 Implementation

Here's a complete, self-contained example using **vanilla JavaScript**:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
  // Select all elements with the class 'target-class'
  const targets = document.querySelectorAll('.target-class');

  // Define the Intersection Observer options
  const options = {
    threshold: 0.1 // Trigger when 10% of the element is visible
  };

  // Create the observer
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries, observer) => {
    entries.forEach(entry => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting && entry.intersectionRatio >= 0.1) {
        // Add the 'active-class' to the element
        entry.target.classList.add('active-class');

        // Optional: Stop observing after the class is added
        observer.unobserve(entry.target);
      }
    });
  }, options);

  // Start observing each target element
  targets.forEach(target => {
    observer.observe(target);
  });
});
```

---

### 📌 Notes

- **`threshold: 0.1`** requests notifications when the intersection ratio crosses 10%. The callback also receives an initial entry; the explicit ratio check makes the class conditional on at least 10% intersection.
- **`observer.unobserve(entry.target)`** ensures the class is only added once, even if the element scrolls in and out of view.
- This script runs after the DOM is fully loaded using `DOMContentLoaded`, ensuring all target elements are available.

---

### 🧪 Example HTML

```html
<div class="target-class">This element will get the 'active-class' when it comes into view.</div>
<div class="target-class">Another element to observe.</div>
```

And in your CSS:

```css
.active-class {
  background-color: yellow;
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
}
```

---

### ✅ Result

When the user scrolls and any `.target-class` element becomes at least 10% visible, the `.active-class` is added to it, and the observer stops watching that element to prevent redundant actions.

This is a clean, efficient, and modern way to handle scroll-based interactions in vanilla JavaScript.

