Yes. Inside a **non-static member function**, you can normally call another non-static private member directly by name:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

class MyClass {
private:
    void privateMethod() {
        std::cout << "Private method called!" << std::endl;
    }

public:
    void publicMethod() {
        privateMethod();       // Uses this object
        // this->privateMethod(); // Equivalent here
    }
};

int main() {
    MyClass obj;
    obj.publicMethod();
    // obj.privateMethod();    // Error: private access
}
```

Access control concerns **where the access occurs**. A member function of `MyClass` may access its private members, including private members of another `MyClass` object. Ordinary outside code cannot do so; friends are an explicit exception.

A static member function also has private access, but it has no `this` object. To call a non-static private method there, supply an object, such as `obj.privateMethod()`. A private static method can be called without an object.

A derived class does not gain access to a base class's private members merely through inheritance.
